WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.173
Allow 4 minutes.

00:00:01.413 --> 00:00:07.387
“Understand triage” communicates a broad intention but does not specify the evidence to collect.

00:00:07.627 --> 00:00:15.584
For this sample, make three elements explicit: the observable behavior, the conditions under which it happens, and the acceptable standard.

00:00:15.824 --> 00:00:22.971
Carnegie Mellon’s learning-objective guidance supports writing objectives that point to a performance that can be assessed.

00:00:23.661 --> 00:00:32.428
Worked example: “Given the current rule card and four fictional support tickets, assign a priority to each ticket with at least three correct.”

00:00:32.428 --> 00:00:34.157
Behavior: assign priorities.

00:00:34.397 --> 00:00:36.765
Condition: four cases and the rule card.

00:00:37.005 --> 00:00:38.775
Standard: three of four correct.

00:00:39.015 --> 00:00:43.602
The numbers are illustrative design choices, not a validated safety threshold.

00:00:43.842 --> 00:00:47.703
A real organization must set standards appropriate to its risks.

00:00:48.393 --> 00:00:52.383
Avoid piling incompatible performances into one objective.

00:00:52.623 --> 00:00:58.319
“List definitions, build an escalation workflow, and motivate colleagues” needs separate evidence.

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:06.793
Also avoid false precision: a strict time limit is appropriate only when speed is genuinely part of the job and the measurement is fair.

00:01:07.483 --> 00:01:16.577
Drafting pause, 60 seconds: rewrite “Know the handoff process.” Sample response: “Given a fictional handoff and the required-field checklist,

00:01:16.577 --> 00:01:21.969
identify all missing required elements.” Add a criterion that reflects the use case.

00:01:22.209 --> 00:01:26.219
Your draft is a self-check, not an automatically verified performance.

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:27.997
Now it's your turn.

00:01:28.237 --> 00:01:31.565
Which objective is most ready to guide a case-based assessment?

00:01:31.805 --> 00:01:34.920
Pause the video and make your choice in the activity below.

